-- 0002_learner_content — the `learner` and `content` schemas, plus one
-- `practice` table that could not wait for its module.
--
-- Generated by drizzle-kit, then reviewed by hand (plan §4, rule 1). The hand
-- pass removed one redundant index, added two missing foreign-key indexes, and
-- appended the COMMENT ON COLUMN block at the foot of this file. No generated
-- DDL was otherwise altered.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT LANDS HERE
--
--   learner   students · student_subjects · chapter_mastery
--   content   chapters · questions · rag_chunks
--   practice  question_responses  (the module is build step 11; see below)
--
-- All three are assigned the `core` connection pool
-- (04-RESILIENCE-PLAN.md §3.1, declared in src/app/module-pools.ts).
-- `retrieval` and `foxy` will READ rag_chunks on the `ai` pool, which is the
-- entire point of §3.1: a slow vector scan cannot hold a connection that a
-- chapter listing — or a login — needs.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- GRADES ARE TEXT. "6" to "12", three tables, three CHECK constraints.
--
-- This is plan §3's hard rule made mechanical. The failure it prevents is not
-- a crash: `"6" !== 6` makes a filter match nothing, so an integer grade
-- surfaces as an empty question list for one cohort and reads as missing
-- content rather than as a bug.
--
-- WHAT THE CHECK DOES AND DOES NOT BUY, established by a test after an earlier
-- draft of this header claimed more than was true:
--
--   IT DOES enforce the VALUE domain. "05", "6 ", "Class 6" and "13" are all
--   refused, in every table, on every write path.
--
--   IT DOES NOT enforce the caller's TYPE. `values (6)` with a bare integer
--   literal SUCCEEDS and stores '6' — Postgres has an assignment cast from
--   integer to text and applies it silently. node-postgres arrives at the same
--   place by another road, sending a JavaScript `6` as an untyped parameter
--   that Postgres infers as text.
--
-- The value in the column is therefore always correct, but "grade 6 as a
-- NUMBER is rejected" (§8.2) is a rule ONLY the module's Zod contract can
-- enforce. That contract is the sole barrier between a JSON number and this
-- column; it is not a convenience wrapper and must not be removed on the
-- belief that the CHECK covers it.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE FOUR-OPTION RULE IS A CONSTRAINT, NOT A VALIDATION.
--
-- §8.3 requires a question with other than 4 options to be rejected.
-- Application validation protects the paths that run it; a CHECK protects the
-- paths nobody remembered — the bulk import, the admin fix-up, the psql
-- session at 2am. Paired with `correct_index` 0..3, the index can never point
-- past the end of the array.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE THREE ONE-WAY DOORS (PROGRESS.md §8). Hours now, unrecoverable later.
--
--   questions.distractor_misconceptions
--       WHY the wrong option was tempting. Without it the parent digest can
--       only report percentages, which is what every competitor reports.
--       Retrofitting means a human re-reading the whole question bank.
--   questions.is_held_out
--       Questions reserved for independent mastery checks, never served in
--       practice. A question that has been served is contaminated forever;
--       you cannot un-serve it. The reserve has to exist before the first
--       student sees the bank.
--   question_responses
--       Every answer, with the difficulty AS AUTHORED AT THE TIME. Real
--       difficulty is only computable from responses recorded when they
--       happened. There is no way to reconstruct them later, so the table
--       lands three build steps ahead of the module that owns it.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- rag_chunks IS SHAPED FOR AN IMPORT, NOT FOR ELEGANCE.
--
-- It is populated by a one-time copy from the existing production table
-- `rag_content_chunks` (~16,000 rows). Every column that exists in both keeps
-- the SOURCE name, type and default, so the import is a column mapping rather
-- than a transform — a transform step is a place for 16,000 rows to be
-- quietly corrupted. `embedding` is vector(1024) because that is exactly what
-- voyage-3 produced for the existing corpus; changing the width invalidates
-- every stored vector and turns a copy into a paid re-embedding run.
--
-- Two deliberate departures the import script must know about:
--   * `chapter_id` is NEW and NULLABLE. Import leaves it null; a backfill
--     joins (grade, subject, chapter_number) to `chapters`. It stays nullable
--     forever — a chunk whose chapter is not in the syllabus table is still
--     retrievable content and must not be lost to a NOT NULL.
--   * `search_vector` is GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED. DO NOT MAP IT. This is
--     the one place the straight mapping is broken on purpose: a
--     hand-maintained tsvector goes stale the first time someone edits
--     chunk_text and forgets, and a stale tsvector does not fail — the chunk
--     simply stops appearing in keyword search, forever, silently.
--
-- Pre-flight before the import, because `grade` carries the "6".."12" CHECK:
--     select distinct grade from rag_content_chunks;
-- A row outside that range is unreachable content (retrieval hard-filters by
-- grade, §8.4 step 3), so failing loudly on it is the correct outcome — but
-- discovering it at 2am mid-import is not. `language` deliberately carries NO
-- check for the same reason inverted: grade is a product invariant, language
-- is a label, and an unexpected 'en-IN' must not block the import.
--
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE HNSW PARAMETERS: m = 16, ef_construction = 128.
--
-- m = 16 is pgvector's default and is ample graph connectivity at this corpus
-- size; raising it costs memory in every query for recall this corpus does not
-- need. ef_construction is DOUBLED from the default 64 because build cost on
-- 16,000 rows is measured in seconds — a one-time cost that is effectively
-- free — while the recall it buys is permanent and cannot be added later
-- without a rebuild.
--
-- Build-time and query-time settings that are NOT in this file because they
-- are session GUCs, and that the import script and the retrieval module
-- respectively must set:
--
--   set maintenance_work_mem = '512MB';   -- before the index build. 16,000 ×
--       1024 × 4 bytes is ~65 MB of vectors; if the graph does not fit,
--       pgvector builds on disk and takes far longer for a worse graph.
--   set hnsw.ef_search = 100;             -- retrieval takes the TOP 50
--       (§8.4 step 3). pgvector's default ef_search is 40, which is BELOW 50,
--       and an HNSW scan will not return more rows than ef_search.
--
--       MEASURED on pgvector 0.8.6, 180 seeded rows, HNSW plan forced:
--           ef_search = 40,  limit 50  ->  40 rows
--           ef_search = 100, limit 50  ->  50 rows
--       Left at the default, retrieval silently receives 40 candidates where
--       it asked for 50. That reads as "the corpus is thin", not as a
--       misconfiguration, which is exactly why it is written down here.
--
-- AND THE ONE THAT WILL BITE: §8.4 hard-filters by grade and subject. An HNSW
-- search walks the graph over the WHOLE table and filters afterwards, so a
-- narrow filter — ~380 rows per (grade, subject) partition at 16,000 rows —
-- can return far fewer than 50 results while looking like a successful query.
-- Set `hnsw.iterative_scan = relaxed_order` (pgvector 0.8+; the container
-- ships 0.8.6, so it is available).
--
-- ALSO MEASURED, and reassuring: at seed scale the planner does NOT choose
-- HNSW for a filtered query at all — it takes a bitmap scan on
-- rag_chunks_grade_subject_idx and sorts exactly. That is the right choice and
-- has perfect recall. It also means the HNSW path is effectively UNTESTED by
-- any small-corpus query, so the filtered-recall behaviour above must be
-- re-measured against the real 16,000-row corpus before the abstention
-- threshold is calibrated. Do not carry these numbers forward as if they
-- settled it.
--
-- The HNSW index is NOT partial, unlike the (grade, subject) filter index.
-- A partial index is unusable by a query that omits its predicate, and the
-- fallback for the vector index is a sequential scan over every embedding —
-- which against the `ai` pool's 5s statement timeout reads as an outage. The
-- filter index can afford that fallback; this one cannot.
-- ===========================================================================

CREATE TABLE "chapter_mastery" (
	"student_user_id" uuid NOT NULL,
	"chapter_id" uuid NOT NULL,
	"mastery_score" numeric(4, 3) DEFAULT '0' NOT NULL,
	"attempts" integer DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL,
	"last_practised_at" timestamp with time zone,
	"updated_at" timestamp with time zone DEFAULT now() NOT NULL,
	CONSTRAINT "chapter_mastery_pkey" PRIMARY KEY("student_user_id","chapter_id"),
	CONSTRAINT "chapter_mastery_score_check" CHECK ("chapter_mastery"."mastery_score" >= 0 and "chapter_mastery"."mastery_score" <= 1),
	CONSTRAINT "chapter_mastery_attempts_check" CHECK ("chapter_mastery"."attempts" >= 0)
);
